# compose.yaml.schema
#
# Companion document for `compose.yaml`, the composition's glue file. Read it
# before editing the file, and read it before merging the file with the parts'
# fragments for the first time.
#
# ## What this file is
#
# It is a Compose override file used as glue, not a service definition. Compose
# lets one deployment be described by several files:
#
#   docker compose -f <first> -f <second> … config
#
# and merges them in the order given. The merge rule, measured on this file
# type with `docker compose config` rather than quoted from memory:
#
#   * a single-value field (`image`, `command`, `entrypoint`, `user`, …) takes
#     the value from the LAST file that sets it;
#   * a map field (`environment`) merges per key, again with the last file
#     winning a key it sets;
#   * a list field (`networks`, and the parts' mount lists) is the UNION of the
#     files' entries, so nothing a part declared is dropped.
#
# Two consequences this composition relies on:
#
#   1. The glue is merged LAST. It declares no single-value field, so a later
#      position cannot be used to override a part — and an earlier position
#      could not protect it from a part either.
#   2. Attaching every service to the shared network is ADDITIVE. A service that
#      a part already attached to its own network keeps that one and gains this
#      one.
#
# ## What each top-level key means
#
# | Key | Meaning |
# |-----|---------|
# | `networks.agent-set.name` | The concrete Docker network name, from `AGENT_SET_NETWORK`. One network for the whole set: it is the only path between the parts that is not a bind mount. |
# | `secrets.<name>.file` | The secret SOURCE: a host file Compose mounts into whichever service references that name. The four names here are contracts with the parts' fragments — a service that references a name nobody declares fails the merge with `secret … not found`, and a name declared here that no service references is dead weight. |
# | `services.<name>.networks` | Network membership only. The service itself is defined by the fragment named in the comment above it. |
#
# ## The parts' fragments this file is merged with
#
# | Fragment | Owns |
# |----------|------|
# | the router's `skeleton/compose.yml` | its service, its port, its own network, and the secret names it references |
# | the store's `skeleton/compose-secrets.yml` | the wiring example for a service's encrypted file and key: two secrets plus the key-path variable. It defines no service of its own — the store is a mechanism |
# Each line names a part's shipped fragment; what the deployment merges is its own
# filled-in copy of each (the Docker-access fragment, for instance, ships with an
# empty `environment:`, which a deployment must complete before the file parses).
# | the Docker-access part's `skeleton/compose-socket-proxy.yml` | its proxy service, the socket mount (into the proxy only), and its network |
# | the host part's `skeleton/compose.service.yaml` | the `agent-host` service: image, entrypoint that starts the multiplexed panes, user, tty, mounts |
# | the harness layer's `skeleton/compose.service.yaml` | the same `agent-host` service with the layer's image and its decrypt-at-boot entrypoint wrapper: last of the two, so its fields win and the host's keep the rest |
#
# ## Parameter placeholders
#
# Every value is a parameter (SCHEMATIC.md § Parameters). The `:?` form is
# deliberate: `${NAME:?message}` makes Compose fail the merge when the variable
# is unset or empty, which is the first of this composition's ordering rules in
# mechanical form — a missing parameter stops the deployment instead of
# producing a container with an empty string in it.
#
# | Placeholder | Parameter |
# |-------------|-----------|
# | `AGENT_SET_NETWORK` | `P-1` |
# | `SECRETS_KEY_DIR` | `P-17` |
# | `SECRETS_STORE_DIR` | `P-18` |
# | `ROUTER_SECRETS_SERVICE` | `P-19` |
# | `AGENT_HOST_SECRETS_SERVICE` | `P-20` |
#
# ## What this file must never contain
#
# `image`, `build`, `command`, `entrypoint`, `environment`, `user`, `ports`,
# `volumes`, `cap_add`, `privileged`, or `devices` under any service. Each of
# those belongs to a part, and a second copy here would silently win the merge
# and drift from the part the pin names. The acceptance row `A-2` fails when one
# appears.
#
# ## Verifying a merge
#
# ```
# docker compose \
#   -f <run-an-llm-router>/skeleton/compose.yml \
#   -f <encrypt-container-secrets>/skeleton/compose-secrets.yml \
#   -f <restrict-docker-api-access>/skeleton/compose-socket-proxy.yml \
#   -f <run-multiplexed-agent-workspaces>/skeleton/compose.service.yaml \
#   -f <add-an-agent-harness>/skeleton/compose.service.yaml \
#   -f compose.yaml \
#   config
# ```
#
# The output is the deployment Compose will actually create. Check three things
# in it: every service of the set is present (`agent-host`, `llm-router`,
# `socket-proxy`), every one of them lists the shared network, and no service's
# `image`, `entrypoint`, or `user` came from this file.
